const AHEAD = [
  { title: 'Legal representations, in fields.', text: `RSL carries warranties as vocabulary of its own: ownership, authority, non-infringement, privacy consent, no malware. It carries disclaimers too, no liability and no indemnity among them ${cite(14)}. In a negotiated license these clauses sit in the contract's prose; RSL puts them where software can read them. FORA handles them the way RSL handles Creative Commons: by reference to a governing document, pinned by its digest, a fingerprint of the document ${cite(28)}. Each approach keeps something the other gives up. A field is machine-readable and narrow; a pinned document is complete and opaque. RSL can state a warranty of ownership in a field of its own; FORA can't.` },
  { title: 'Terms inside the file, and terms that enforce themselves.', text: `RSL can embed the license in the file itself, in XMP, ID3 or EPUB metadata, so an EPUB carries its license into any hands, with no counterparty involved at all. Its encrypted-media option makes access possible "only for clients holding a valid License Token" ${cite(14)}. Misuse is then mechanically impossible, not something to argue under a contract. FORA enforces access at the edge, with a signed link, and the terms after delivery by reconciling the records of each sale ${cite(22)}.` },
  { title: 'A sale without an Exchange.', text: `An RSL file can name a license server that issues a token for the license a client picks, and can name x402 as the way to pay ${cite(14)}. FORA leaves money to the Exchange operator, which bills the buyer, and its protocol names no payment rail ${cite(22)}.` },
  { title: 'A description of the content.', text: `CoMP describes what is being sold: its category, its provenance, its word count, and whether a human, a model or both produced it ${cite(20)}. FORA's core has no field for any of it; it carries CoMP's fields whole on each offer ${cite(22)}. CoMP also splits AI use into sub-functions, among them RAG, grounding, agent view and agent actions, where FORA's use words stop at <code>ai-input</code> ${cite(20, 28)}.` },
  { title: 'A file you write by hand.', text: `RSL's quickstart is one robots.txt line and an eleven-line XML file; a publisher can write it in a minute ${cite(17)}. FORA is a machine-validated schema that an Exchange operator implements, and the publisher sets its terms in a console.` },
];

const DIFFER = [
  { title: 'A price and a credit together.', text: `In RSL, a license asks for money or for credit, not both. Its schema (RSL 1.0, Appendix A) allows one payment element with one type, and <code>attribution</code> is one of the types ${cite(14)}. CoMP keeps citation in a field of its own, beside the price ${cite(20)}. A FORA term can charge per access and require credit ${cite(28)}.` },
  { title: 'Caps.', text: `FORA states a cap as a metric, a limit and a window: ten seats a month, say. The buyer agrees to it; the Exchange doesn't meter it ${cite(28)}. RSL's schema has no cap element (Appendix A) ${cite(14)}. CoMP has a yes-or-no field that says a crawl limit exists ${cite(20)}.` },
  { title: 'A menu for one asset.', text: `A FORA resource carries up to 32 terms, and each one a buyer may see is its own signed offer ${cite(28)}. RSL allows several licenses for the same content, but tells processors to "evaluate all applicable licensing terms together" ${cite(14)}. A CoMP response carries one package with one price, as of November 14, 2026 ${cite(20)}.` },
  { title: 'Saying what you want first.', text: `CoMP lets an AI system state its intended use ${cite(20)}. FORA lets a buyer state the uses, places and kind of user it accepts, and an Exchange may return only the offers that fit ${cite(28)}.` },
];

