    name: 'Others mentioned',
    items: [
      { title: 'Supertab Connect', publisher: 'Supertab', href: 'https://www.supertab.co/', checked: '2026-11-13' },
      { title: 'Tokens', publisher: 'TollBit docs', href: 'https://docs.tollbit.com/docs/tokens', checked: '2026-11-14' },
      { title: 'Daily Kos\'s RSL file', publisher: 'Daily Kos', href: 'https://www.dailykos.com/license.xml', checked: '2026-11-13' },
      { title: 'IPTC Photo Metadata Standard', publisher: 'IPTC', href: 'https://iptc.org/std/photometadata/specification/IPTC-PhotoMetadata', checked: '2026-11-15' },
      { title: 'ODRL Information Model 2.2', publisher: 'W3C', href: 'https://www.w3.org/TR/odrl-model/', checked: '2026-11-15' },
      { title: 'Stakeholder consultation on AI and copyright compliance', publisher: 'European Commission', href: 'https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/stakeholder-consultation-ai-and-copyright-compliance', checked: '2026-11-15' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Contracts: the search-only contract',
    items: [
      { title: 'Search Only Terms Contract', publisher: 'Movement for an Open Web', href: S.socw, checked: '2026-11-14' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<LandingLayout
  title="The standards map — FORA resources"
  description="Thirteen open standards tell a machine something about your content. What each one does, which two move money, how they stack, and where FORA sits among them."
>
  <AudienceHero
    current="resources"
    badge="Resources · Standards"
    title="What each AI standard does. <em>Thirteen standards, eight jobs.</em>"
    sub="Thirteen open standards tell AI systems something about your content: what it is, who may fetch it, what may be done with it, what it costs, and what happened after. In a vendor pitch they can sound interchangeable. They aren't. They do eight different jobs, and they stack. Two of them move money: x402 and MPP."
    updated={resourceUpdated(HREF)}
  >
    <a href="#standards" class="btn btn-primary">See the thirteen</a>
    <a href="/resources/ai-crawlers/#generator" class="btn btn-ghost">Generate your rules</a>
  </AudienceHero>

  <Band tone="near" title="Declaring is not the same as being paid">
    <Callout kind="takeaway" tag="Key takeaway">
      <p class="takeaway">A machine-readable declaration is a request, not a control. TDMRep reserves rights that bind in the EU and that no technical layer enforces. A declaration earns money when something stands behind it: a license server, an Exchange, or a contract that access triggers.</p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="prose ask"><b>Ask your vendors.</b> Put this to your CDN, bot-management vendor or marketplace, verbatim: <em>"Which of these standards do you read, which do you enforce at the door, and who is paying under any of them through you today?"</em></p>
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.exchange} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="standards" title="Thirteen standards, eight jobs" lead={`A machine that buys content has to find it, identify itself, learn the terms, pay and report what it did. Each standard below does one or two of those jobs. The companies that sell access are on ${a('/resources/content-marketplaces/', 'Content marketplaces')}, which also explains ${a('/resources/content-marketplaces/#broker-order', 'how a Broker orders offers')} for agents.`}>
    <Split aside="320px">
      <StandardsMapTable rows={STANDARDS} label="Thirteen standards, eight jobs" />
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src={`${IMG}/standards-1.png`}
        title="Thirteen standards, eight jobs."
        alt="Eight layers, each a different job: declare terms (robots.txt, AIPREF, TDMRep, RSL, CoMP, FORA), help discovery (llms.txt), identify the caller (Web Bot Auth), connect (MCP), prove origin (C2PA), transact (RSL's license server, FORA), settle, which moves money (x402, MPP), and report (SPUR Content Telemetry, FORA's usage reports)."
      />
    </Split>
    <p class="prose"><b>How to read it.</b> Each row is a different job, so read across a row, not down a column: a road sign isn't worse than a till for not taking payment. The last column mixes kinds of adoption, and each cell says which.</p>
    <p class="prose">Standards in different layers aren't alternatives. Transacting agrees one sale; settling moves the money. RSL spans two layers: it declares and, through its license server, transacts. FORA spans the terms, the transaction and the report, and leaves the money to your operator.</p>
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.operator} />
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.signedLink} />
  </Band>

  <Band title="Where each job stands, November 2026">
    <KeyFacts items={KEY_FACTS} />
    <PointList items={STANDING} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" title="What to do this quarter">
    <PointList numbered items={TODO} />
    <Callout kind="define" {...DEFINITIONS.edgeFunction} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="search-only" title="Charging through your site's terms: the search-only contract">
    <Split aside="400px">
      <div>
        <p class="prose" set:html={`The Movement for an Open Web publishes the ${a(S.socw, 'Search Only Terms Contract')}, a legal text a publisher points to from robots.txt and incorporates into its site terms. Indexing for search and a person's own reading are free. A fee is owed for "scraping, bulk extraction, dataset creation, or any AI-related use", for each article, image, video or audio file, at the amount the publisher sets.`} />
        <p class="prose" set:html={`A contract covers what the edge function can't see: your open pages, and crawlers that hide what they are. ${a('/getting-started/search-only-contract/', 'How to adopt the contract →')}`} />
      </div>
      <Callout slot="aside" kind="legal" points={LEGAL} />
    </Split>
  </Band>

  <Band id="proof" title="Proof the buyer agreed to your terms">
    <p class="prose" set:html={`FORA asks more of you than an RSL file, which a publisher can write in a minute ${cite(17)}. FORA spans the terms, the transaction and the report, which is why it needs an Exchange and keys. For a publisher choosing what to do this quarter, that difference may matter more than every comparison below the divider.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`What FORA gives back is proof. You set several terms for a page: free for search, a price per AI answer, a yearly deal for one licensee. The Exchange signs an offer for each term the buyer may see. The buyer signs the one it picked, with its own registered key. The Exchange issues a signed link against that acceptance, and both sides keep the record ${cite(23, 28)}.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`A buyer that says it didn't know the terms has its own signature on them. You can check it in the Exchange operator's publisher console, which verifies both signatures in your browser ${cite(27)}. None of the other license formats carries the buyer's own signature on the terms it accepted. How RSL's license server compares is <a href="#license-server">below</a>.`} />
  </Band>

  <div class="divider-band">
    <div class="divider-wrap">
      <SectionDivider label="For your legal and product teams" />
      <p class="divider-note">The rest of the page compares the specifications clause by clause. Everything above reads without it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Band tone="near" title="What a license file can say: uses, price, place and duties">
    <p class="prose">Three of the thirteen are license formats: RSL 1.0, CoMP and FORA's licensing core. All three say what you may do, who may buy, where, at what price, and what you owe afterward. TDMRep and AIPREF say only the first.</p>
    <p class="prose" set:html={`On uses they speak almost the same language. RSL defines six use words, from <code>all</code> to <code>search</code> ${cite(14)}. CoMP takes the same six names, four definitions word for word ${cite(20)}. FORA starts from RSL's use words and adds copyright uses such as reproduce and sync, 23 in all. It also accepts AIPREF's <code>train-ai</code> as a spelling of <code>ai-train</code> ${cite(28)}. RSL and FORA both scope a term by place and kind of user, and both can prohibit as well as permit ${cite(14, 28)}.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`On what you owe afterward they part. RSL's reporting rule: a client that "does not recognize or cannot comply with the profile MUST treat that activity as not licensed" ${cite(14)}. CoMP has a citation flag and a reporting address ${cite(20)}. FORA has obligations, credit and notices among them ${cite(28)}. An unknown RSL element "MUST render the document non-conformant", so one element a client doesn't know affects the whole file ${cite(14)}. A FORA agent that can't evaluate a term declines only that term ${cite(28)}.`} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="ahead" title="Where RSL and CoMP are ahead" lead="The comparison cuts both ways.">
    <PointList items={AHEAD} />
  </Band>

  <Band title="Where the three license formats differ">
    <p class="prose" set:html={`RSL's <code>&lt;standard&gt;</code> element points at a legal text elsewhere, such as a Creative Commons license ${cite(14)}. FORA incorporates by reference too, with the document's digest beside its address, so the document can't be swapped after an offer is signed ${cite(28)}.`} />
    <PointList items={DIFFER} />
    <p class="prose explorer" set:html={`<b>See it clause by clause.</b> The ${a('/protocol/#licensing-core', 'Licensing Core explorer')} shows 12 licenses, each as a FORA term, an RSL file and a CoMP package.`} />
    <CardGrid items={EXPLORER} />
    <LinkRow links={[{ href: '/protocol/#licensing-core', text: 'Open the explorer' }]} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="license-server" title="How RSL sells: a license server" asideWidth="400px" asideAlign="start">
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <p class="prose" set:html={`RSL is more than a declaration. Its License Server "Implements the Open License Protocol (OLP) to issue license tokens and enforces payment requirements on behalf of Publishers". A license token is the pass that server issues; a site or CDN can ask which license and asset it covers ${cite(14)}.`} />
      <p class="prose" set:html={`The difference that matters in a dispute is who signs. RSL's client doesn't sign the license its token covers, and <code>&lt;standard&gt;</code> takes an address with no digest (Appendix A) ${cite(14)}. TollBit's content tokens name a license type and a license ID, and TollBit signs them, not the buyer ${cite(58)}. CoMP records how a crawler plans to identify itself, which is not its assent ${cite(20)}.`} />
      <p class="prose" set:html={`Supertab sells what it calls an "RSL-compliant license server", and Daily Kos's RSL file names it ${cite(57, 59)}.`} />
      <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>What ILC does with it.</b> ILC, the collective that co-authored RSL, keeps the transaction out of the content path ${cite(16, 18)}. Its RSL file names no license server and points, through <code>&lt;standard&gt;</code>, at ILC's collective license ${cite(19)}. An AI company takes one blanket license, for a fee ILC doesn't publish, and reports its own use ${cite(18)}. Yahoo Scout, ILC's first licensee, pays under that contract ${cite(16)}. ${a('/resources/transparent-collective/', 'How ILC compares with other collectives →')}`} />
    </Fragment>
    <Figure
      slot="aside"
      src={`${IMG}/standards-3.png`}
      title="Two ways to transact: RSL's license server and FORA's Exchange."
      alt="Two sequences side by side. RSL license server: your RSL file names a license server; the client asks for one exact license; the server issues a License Token; the file can name x402 as the way to pay; the client doesn't sign the license its token covers. FORA Exchange: you set several terms for a page; the Exchange signs an offer for each; the buyer signs the one it picked (proof the buyer agreed to your terms); a signed link is issued against that acceptance; both sides keep the record. The operator bills the buyer, and FORA names no payment rail."
    />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" title="Provenance is not licensing: where C2PA fits">
    <p class="prose" set:html={`C2PA defines signed provenance metadata bound to a file: who made it, with what tool, and what has been done to it since. Content Credentials is the consumer name ${cite(40)}. Adobe, Arm, BBC, Intel, Microsoft and Truepic founded it in 2021 ${cite(45)}.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`Provenance proves where content came from; licensing governs what may be done with it. C2PA's core specification removed its training-and-mining assertion in version 2.0; the Creator Assertions Working Group's specification now carries that signal ${cite(41, 46)}. A publisher who adopts C2PA has licensed nothing. It has evidence: a signed manifest with a content hash shows what was delivered and whether it changed. FORA carries C2PA validation results as a signed attestation about a resource ${cite(22)}. Text has been in the specification since 2.3, but if text "is only partially copied", its manifest "may be truncated or absent" ${cite(41)}.`} />
  </Band>

  <Band title="How the standards plug into each other">
    <Split aside="380px">
      <div>
        <Callout kind="engineers" collapse="Show the details" points={ENGINEERS} />
        <p class="prose" set:html={`<b>Two pairs that can contradict each other.</b> RSL states which uses are permitted; CoMP leaves uses to the license it links, so the two can disagree unseen ${cite(14, 20)}. If you publish both, check that they say the same thing. robots.txt governs fetching by crawler name and TDMRep reserves a use. The TDMRep group calls them complementary, but "this complementarity is not specified in the TDMRep document and remains subject to interpretation" ${cite(13)}.`} />
      </div>
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src={`${IMG}/standards-5.png`}
        title="Where FORA plugs into the other standards."
        alt="Four standards feed into FORA: CoMP's fields travel on each offer, C2PA's results arrive as an attestation, an MCP assistant reaches FORA through the Identity Service, and FORA's request signatures are a profile of Web Bot Auth. An Exchange operator can collect over x402 or MPP; FORA Server ships no adapter for either (November 2026)."
      />
    </Split>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" title="What you can learn about use after delivery">
    <p class="prose" set:html={`SPUR's Content Telemetry 1.0 specifies "a telemetry schema for tracking AI agent content usage from retrieval through user engagement". Your server or CDN can report retrieval "with no cooperation from the AI agent required"; only the AI company can report grounding, citation and presentation ${cite(55)}. RSL's reporting element can point at a profile like this one ${cite(14)}.`} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={`In FORA, the buyer reports its usage to the Exchange that sold the item, and each report names the transaction it covers ${cite(22)}. For disputes and payouts, see <a href="#about">About FORA</a>.`} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" title="Questions">
    <FaqGrid items={FAQ} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="about">
    <Callout kind="about" points={ABOUT_FORA} columns />
    <section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-label">
      <h2 class="end-label" id="sources-label">Sources</h2>
      <details class="all-sources" id="all-sources">
        <summary>All sources</summary>
        <SourceList groups={SOURCES} compact numbered />
      </details>
      <p class="note">Sources are checked on the date of publication.</p>
    </section>
    <SeriesNav current={HREF} />
  </Band>

  <ClosingCta
    title="Declare what you sell. <em>Then make it purchasable.</em>"
    text="Generate your robots.txt and headers, attach the search-only contract, and see what an agent would be offered for one of your pages."
  >
    <a href="/resources/ai-crawlers/#generator" class="btn btn-primary">Generate your rules</a>
    <a href="/publishers/" class="btn btn-ghost">See your offers</a>
  </ClosingCta>
</LandingLayout>

<script is:inline>
  // The sources sit in a closed <details>: a citation marker, or a link to
  // #src-N, opens it before the browser scrolls to the entry.
  (function () {
    var box = document.getElementById('all-sources');
    if (!box) return;
    function open() {
      if (/^#src-\d+$/.test(location.hash) && !box.open) {
        box.open = true;
        var el = document.getElementById(location.hash.slice(1));
        if (el) el.scrollIntoView();
      }
    }
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', open);
    // A second click on the same marker fires no hashchange.
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var link = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('a[href^="#src-"]');
      if (link) box.open = true;
    });
    open();
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* Prose between components. The same few lines as on the TollBit page. */
  .prose { color: var(--lp-text); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 44rem; }
  .prose + .prose { margin-top: 1rem; }
  :global(*) + .prose { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .prose :global(a) { color: var(--lp-link); }
  .prose :global(b) { color: var(--lp-strong); font-weight: 600; }
  .prose :global(code) { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.875em; color: var(--lp-heading); }
  .prose em { color: var(--lp-heading); }
  .ask { margin-top: 2rem; }
  /* Band's lead doesn't colour links; the table's lead holds two. */
  :global(.band-lead a) { color: var(--lp-link); }
  .explorer { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  /* The key takeaway, drawn large like a pull quote, in FORA's voice. */
  .takeaway { font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.2vw, 1.5rem) !important; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--lp-heading); font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }

  /* The section divider sits between bands, on the page's own black. */
  .divider-band {
    --lp-rule: var(--mid);
    --lp-label: var(--light-gray);
    background: var(--black);
    padding: 0 2rem 3.5rem;
  }
  .divider-wrap { max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .divider-note { margin: 0.75rem 0 0 3rem; color: var(--gray); font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; }

  .sources { margin-top: 4rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .end-label { font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--lp-label); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .all-sources > summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--lp-link); font-weight: 600; }
  .note { max-width: 52rem; margin-top: 1.25rem; color: var(--lp-muted); font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .divider-band { padding: 0 1rem 2.75rem; }
  }
</style>
